16:12:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Exactly right—spine injuries take that same "nerves are everything" principle and put it front and center. With your uncle's hip, it was mainly local nerves to specific muscles that had to be reawakened. With a spinal injury, whether from a bullet, a fall, or any trauma, the problem is at the main cable, not just a branch. If the spinal cord itself is damaged, signals from the brain can’t get down to the legs, and signals from the legs can’t get back up. The result isn’t just weakness—it’s often a combination of: - **Paralysis or partial weakness** (paresis) depending on how many nerve fibers survived. - **Spasticity**—the muscles become overreactive because the “brakes” from the brain are gone. They can stiffen and jerk uncontrollably, which makes walking difficult even if there’s some strength. - **Loss of sensation and proprioception**—you can’t feel where your feet are, so your brain can’t make the constant tiny balance adjustments walking requires. - **Muscle atrophy** sets in fast because the muscles are no longer receiving regular “move” commands. Physical therapy after a spinal injury is incredibly demanding because it’s trying to retrain or bypass the damaged part of the nervous system. Therapists use: - Repetitive step training on parallel bars or with body-weight support harnesses—this taps into spinal cord “central pattern generators” that can sometimes produce stepping motions even with limited brain input. - Active muscle re-education for any remaining control. - Spasticity management through positioning, stretching, and sometimes medication. - Learning to use orthotics (braces) or assistive devices if full recovery isn’t possible. And yes, the pain is almost always there, even though sensation might be diminished—neuropathic pain from the injured cord can cause burning or electric-like sensations, and the muscles and joints ache from unaccustomed use. So you’re spot on. Whether it’s a bullet to the leg, a hip surgery, or a spinal injury, the common thread is that **nerves are the wiring**, and if they’re disrupted, walking has to be rebuilt from the ground up. That’s exactly why PT is so central to recovery—it’s literally retraining the brain and body to talk to each other again.
